 The main contributions are:
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item[(1)] \textbf{Ranking precision changes the interpretation of an apparent budget effect.} On ca-GrQc, tightening PageRank tolerance exchanges two of 20 candidates. The default mean gain-to-loss reversal then disappears, although budget-dependent seed changes remain. This post-hoc control shows that the reversal cannot be interpreted as a reference-budget effect independent of ranking precision.
-\item[(2)] \textbf{Reference-set instability can accompany a small average spread improvement.} Increasing $W$ from 1280 to 5120 changes 16 of 40 contact-graph reference sets. The mean independently evaluated spread increase is 0.01111 nodes across all 40 network--path pairs. These measurements distinguish the frequency of seed-set changes from the magnitude of the resulting utility changes.
-\item[(3)] \textbf{Degree and PageRank agreement diverge on the external networks.} At $W=1280$, Degree-4k reproduces all 15 full-reference trajectories using 20 candidates and less selection time in this implementation. Default PageRank-4k reproduces none; losses on CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote persist under tighter tolerance. The comparison identifies limits to the earlier contact-graph agreement and ties the observed cost savings to preservation of the tested reference.
+\item[(1)] \textbf{We identify how ranking precision alters an apparent budget effect.} Through paired budget comparisons and a post-hoc precision control, we distinguish the observed quality reversal from persistent budget-dependent seed changes. On ca-GrQc, tightening PageRank tolerance exchanges two of 20 candidates and removes the default mean gain-to-loss reversal. This identifies a numerical dependence that a budget scan at default tolerance alone would miss.
+\item[(2)] \textbf{We quantify the spread gains associated with changes in the reference seed set.} Independent paired evaluation measures the magnitude and conditional Monte Carlo uncertainty of these gains. In the historical comparison from $W=1280$ to $W=5120$, 16 of 40 reference sets change, while mean spread increases by 0.01111 nodes across all 40 network--path pairs. The measurements separate seed-set instability from the utility gained through additional sampling.
+\item[(3)] \textbf{We identify which tested candidate pools preserve the reference while reducing selection cost.} At $W=1280$, Degree-4k uses 20 candidates to reproduce all 15 external reference trajectories at lower selection cost in this implementation. Default PageRank-4k reproduces none, and losses on CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote persist under tighter tolerance. These comparisons delimit the transfer of earlier contact-graph agreement and relate computational savings to independently evaluated utility.
 \end{enumerate}
 
